<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Imperialism, the Newest Stage of Capitalism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a>, the Highest Stage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">Capitalism</a> is a book by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, written in Zürich in 1916 and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> in 1917, originally as Imperialism, the Newest Stage of Capitalism. It argues that capitalism had moved from competition to monopoly, that the merger of bank and industrial capital produced finance capital and a financial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, and that the export of capital to undeveloped countries divided the world among the great powers. Lenin presented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> as a predatory war among empires. He drew on Imperialism: A Study (1902) by John A. Hobson and Finance Capital (1910) by Rudolf Hilferding, and rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a>'s theory of ultra-imperialism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism.jpg" alt="Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism" width="330" height="492" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Filimonov Ivan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ленин_В._И._Империализм,_как_высшая_стадия_капитализма_(1917).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Lenin wrote the work between January and June 1916 and sent it to Parus, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a> publishing house founded by the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gorky">Maxim Gorky</a>. It was meant to belong to a series of popular surveys of the West European countries involved in the war, edited by the Marxist historian Mikhail Pokrovsky, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Gorky</a> issued it in addition to the regular series. It was announced in Gorky's magazine Letopis and published by Zhizn i Znaniye in mid-1917, with a preface by Lenin dated 26 April and with his criticisms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Karl Kautsky</a> removed.</p><p>After the war Lenin added a new preface, dated 6 July 1920, for the French and German editions; it first appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a>, No. 18, in 1921. The preface to those editions held that revolt against the capitalist world system would come through the colonies and semi-colonies, with their &quot;thousand million people&quot;, rather than through the urban workers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book has ten chapters. The first three trace how the concentration of production produces monopolies, how banks cease to be mere intermediaries and come to control industry, and how the merger of bank and industrial capital creates finance capital and a financial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>. The fourth chapter argues that surplus capital is exported to less developed countries in search of higher profits, and the fifth and sixth describe the division of the world into colonies and spheres of influence, which Lenin dated to the final partition between 1876 and 1900.</p><p>Lenin held that older powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had taken most colonial possessions while newer ones such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> grew faster, and that only a war of redivision could resolve the contradiction. In the seventh chapter he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-kautsky">Kautsky</a>'s ultra-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, the idea that international cartels would soften rivalry among the powers. The eighth chapter describes the parasitism of rentier states and the bribing of an upper layer of the working class with monopoly profits. The last chapters survey attitudes to imperialism and call it a moribund, transitional stage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Lenin expected to put the theory into practice through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Third International</a> (1919-1943), whose conferences of July and August 1920 he dominated intellectually and politically. The 1920 edition cited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> of 1918 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1919 as proof that empire and hegemony, not nationalism, lay behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Kwame Nkrumah took inspiration from the text for his work on neocolonialism, and the title of his book Neo-Colonialism, the Last Stage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a> alludes to it. Editions appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1948, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> in 2000 and as a Penguin Classic in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vladimir Lenin, drawing on Rudolf Hilferding’s Finance Capital (1910), called the banks “the chief nerve centres” of the capitalist system in <b>Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</b> (1917).</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Vladimir Lenin built on Hobson in <b>Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</b> (1916), portraying imperialism as monopoly capitalism on a world scale that made imperialist wars inevitable; the treatise remained a standard textbook until the Soviet collapse.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
